{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791566395,"nonce":"v1VNTO+nVilOUutRckIvfeERMUw=","title":"Reactive items cost 1.4-1.8 KB each: handles and callbacks held through N-API","message":"A computed or an effect costs about 1.4-1.8 KB of process memory, against about 0.2 KB for the same closure kept in a JavaScript array. Most of it is not the graph: it is how each item's handle and callback cross N-API. A UI holds thousands of these (one or more per bound property), so it is a large share of an app's memory.\n\n**Measured** (2026-10-09, Node 24.2.0, M-series Mac, blinc_ts at e0eb06a with its release addon; per item, process footprint with footprint -p, after GC):\n- Per item, alive: closure in a JS array 214 B; signal 730 B; computed 1,397-1,759 B; effect 1,717 B (V8 heap 105, 236, 444-484, 564 B of those).\n- blinc_abi's graph alone (Rust, counting allocator): 77 B a signal, 188 a computed, 120 an effect while alive; after removal 104-220 B per item stays as slot capacity, the same every round, so reused, not leaked.\n- Not a leak: over six rounds of making and disposing 100,000 items, footprint levels off after two or three rounds once N-API finalizers have run (Node runs them on a later tick after a GC). Measured straight after GC it looked like growth.\n- The rest, split with a standalone addon (200,000 items): closure in a JS array 169 B; the same held by an N-API function reference 351 B; plus a boxed Rust closure owning it, as the graph keeps a callback, 452 B; a plain JS object holding a number 71 B; a native object wrapped for JavaScript (a napi class instance, as each Signal, Computed and Effect handle is) 677 B.\n\n**Plan**\n1. Handles are numbers. Graph items are named by a generation-checked key (index and generation in one number below 2^53) that the TypeScript Signal, Computed and Effect objects hold; the native graph object takes keys (get, set, dispose). No napi class instance per item: about 600 B less each, and disposal frees native memory at once instead of waiting for a finalizer.\n2. Callbacks stay in JavaScript. The context keeps its callbacks in a JavaScript array indexed by id, and the native graph stores the id. One dispatcher function per context is the only N-API reference. About 280 B less per computed or effect (a reference and a boxed closure).\n3. One call per flush. When effects are due, native returns their ids (a Uint32Array) and JavaScript runs them, rather than native calling into JavaScript once per effect. A computed's evaluation stays synchronous, through the dispatcher.\nExpected: a computed or effect at about 0.5 KB (graph node, JS closure, TS object), a third of today. Check with the same measurement, and add it to benchmarks/ beside the change.\n\nThe measurement scripts and the addon: scratchpad (closures.mjs, rounds2.mjs, holdmem.mjs); to move under benchmarks/ with the change.","files":null}]}